.TH CVEC_INIT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_init_mp \- create the vector behind a declared handle with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_init_mp(v, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a vector and stores it in v. It does this exactly as
cvec_init(3) does. The vector takes every allocation that it makes from
mprocs, and not from the default allocator. cvec_declare(3) or
cvec_declare_scoped(3) must declare v first. The element size comes from
the element type in that declaration.

The vector keeps a private copy of the procedure table. The
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t object here therefore does not need to live longer
than the vector. The functions in that table do need to live that long. The
vector frees the backing buffer, the copy of the table and the container
itself through those functions, when it is destroyed. A NULL mprocs is the
same as a call to cvec_init(3).

The new vector holds no elements. It starts at the minimum capacity of 4.
The capacity doubles when a push finds the buffer full. It halves when the
element count falls below a quarter of it. It never falls below that
minimum of 4.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable for the new vector. It must be a
        plain identifier. cvec_declare(3) or cvec_declare_scoped(3) must
        declare it first, and its companion type variables must still be
        in scope.
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector. The message names v and the error below it. Use
cvector_create_full(3) directly to handle a failed creation yourself.
That function does not stop the program.
The macro rejects a procedure table with any of its four members left as
NULL. That rejection is one of the failures that stops the program here.
The macro expands to a do/while block. It needs a semicolon at the end of
the call site.
The macro evaluates v more than once, and it pastes v into the names of
the companion variables. v must therefore be a plain identifier, and
never an expression. The macro evaluates mprocs one time.
cvector_get_mprocs(3) reads back the table that created a vector.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

cvec_declare(my_vec, int);
cvec_init_mp(my_vec, &procs);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3),
.BR cvector_create_full (3),
.BR cvector_get_mprocs (3)
